WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.470
We're still looking for a way of
dealing with non-additive matrices.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.950
So in this section, let's try and
develop a heuristic.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.900
In practice, researchers often assume

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.670
that every internal node of a phylogeny
corresponds to a speciation event,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.490
which is defined as an event where
one species divides into two.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.020
In this case, we need to place limits
on the internal nodes of the tree.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.820
For example, if we're working with
unrooted trees, our requirement is that

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.165
every internal node
needs to have degree 3.

00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:41.035
This means that regardless
of where the root is,

00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:45.725
when we progress from the root to a leaf,
every time we encounter an internal node,

00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:47.155
the tree splits into two pieces.

00:00:48.775 --> 00:00:53.720
For example, if we place the root on
the limb of the squirrel monkey here,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.950
then as we progress through the tree,
it splits into two pieces,

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.990
splits, splits, and so on.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.844
If we root the tree, then, at that location,

00:01:04.844 --> 00:01:07.803
we obtain the following
rooted binary tree.

00:01:11.216 --> 00:01:15.582
Our goal, then, is to design
a heuristic that models

00:01:15.582 --> 00:01:19.960
a "molecular clock" that
assigns each leaf an age.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.240
The age of each leaf node is 0.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.250
And then we need to assign
ages to the internal nodes.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.660
The age of an internal node is
going to represent how long ago

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:34.710
the speciation event represented
at that internal node occurred.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.090
So for this monkey phylogeny,
the ages are in millions of years.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.180
Once we assign ages to the nodes
of a rooted binary tree,

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.990
we get the weights of the edges
of this tree (here shown in red)

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.480
simply by taking the difference
between the ages.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.290
So for example,
the age of this node is 33.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.120
The age of that node is 23.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.563
We get the edge weight 10 for
free just by subtracting the two ages.

00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:08.885
Furthermore, if we assign ages
to the nodes of the tree,

00:02:08.885 --> 00:02:13.620
then it automatically implies that
the tree is what's called "ultrametric",

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.490
meaning that the length of every path
from the root to a leaf is the same.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:21.080
So, here the age of the root
is 33 million years, and

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.100
the length of any path from the root
to a leaf node is 33.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.730
The heuristic that
we're going to introduce for

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.330
constructing an ultrametric,
rooted binary tree

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.020
is called "UPGMA",

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.420
which stands for "Unweighted Pair
Group Method with Arithmetic Mean".

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.060
UPGMA is going to construct
an evolutionary tree

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.590
by clustering the species from
the distance matrix into larger and

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.500
larger clusters,
beginning with single element clusters.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.610
At each step, it looks for
the two closest clusters,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.280
according to the average
distance among all

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.870
pairs of elements taken
from the two clusters.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.350
Now, at this stage of the algorithm, we're
dealing with single element clusters, so

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.120
if we're looking for the closest clusters,
that's just the smallest element

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.560
of the distance matrix,
which corresponds to k and l.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.960
Now, I hope you're worried because
we've seen the perils of choosing

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.730
the minimum element of
a distance matrix as neighbors.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.160
But UPGMA is a heuristic, and

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.920
it's not going to break like
adding additive phylogeny did.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.198
So once we've found the two
closest clusters, C1 and

00:03:28.198 --> 00:03:31.520
C2, we can merge them
into a single cluster, C.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.020
Here we put k and l into a cluster
together because they're the closest.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.950
We then conform a node for C and
connect that node to both C1 and C2.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.570
We then set the age of this internal node

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.250
equal to half of this average
distance between C1 and C2.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.250
So we saw that the distance
from k to l was 2,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.955
so the age of this node is just 1.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.720
We then recompute the average distance
between each pair of clusters and

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.240
we form an updated distance matrix.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.380
Now, we then just begin this process over,

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.060
choosing a minimum element of our matrix,

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.200
merging the two associated clusters,
forming a new node for

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.120
the merged clusters (which here is (i,j))

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.400
and then assigning the age of this node
as half of the distance value from

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.540
the matrix, which was 3 here,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.870
which gives us an age of 1.5.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.350
After one more update
of the distance matrix,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.150
our only choice is to merge
the remaining two clusters,

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.840
assigning an age of 2, and
this yields the final UPGMA tree.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.480
Now, here is the outline of UPGMA
provided on a single slide.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.045
Please feel free to just pause the video
here if you need a little more

00:04:50.045 --> 00:04:51.405
time to understand the algorithm.

00:04:56.049 --> 00:04:58.809
You should now be ready
to implement UPGMA and

00:04:58.809 --> 00:05:00.810
apply it to the coronavirus data.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:08.060
Of course, Son will complain because we
never have really stated a computational

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.060
problem that UPGMA is trying to
provide an approximate solution for,

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.420
but that's okay.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:18.680
The goal of UPGMA is not to
fit a distance matrix, but

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.090
to provide a reliable method that always
can construct an ultrametric tree,

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.050
regardless of what the input data is.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:31.680
So, we know that the tree produced by
UPGMA can't possibly fit this matrix,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.620
because the matrix is non-additive.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.770
You can see, for example, that
the distance from i to l is 3 in

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.100
the distance matrix, but
4 according to the UPGMA tree.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.010
So in summary,
we've encountered two methods for

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.670
constructing an evolutionary
tree from a distance matrix.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.070
Additive Phylogeny works if you give
it an additive matrix as input.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:06:02.020
But then, on the other hand, it fails
completely on non-additive matrices.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:07.470
Then, UPGMA, in contrast, will produce
a tree for any input that you like,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.700
but the tree produced doesn't necessarily
fit an additive matrix in a natural way.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.980
So, can we possibly hope for
an algorithm, one single algorithm,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.310
that's going to combine the better
features of both of these algorithms,

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:27.210
in that it always produces the tree
that fits an additive matrix, but

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.320
it also offers a good heuristic for
non-additive matrices?

